# Level 4 → 5 — Identifying the Human-Readable File

## Level Goal
Find the only human-readable file among ten files in the `inhere` directory.

## Description
The `inhere` directory contains ten files (`-file00` through `-file09`), only one of which contains human-readable text. The `file` command identifies file content based on magic bytes rather than file extensions.

## Approach
My initial approach was manual — opening each file one by one:

```bash
cat ./-file00
cat ./-file01
# and so on...
```

Most returned garbled binary output. The efficient solution was to run `file` on all files at once using shell globbing:

```bash
file ./*
```

This immediately revealed that `-file07` was the only one identified as ASCII text. I then read it:

```bash
cat ./-file07
```

> Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]

## Key Takeaway
Never rely on file extensions to determine file type. The `file` command reads magic bytes to determine a file's true format. This is fundamental in malware analysis and digital forensics.
